## Summary

Write TypeScript code strictly adhering to the following rules:

**Formatting**
Use 1 tab per indent level. Use double quotes for string literals; use template literals only for interpolation or multi-line strings. Never use single quotes. Terminate all statements with semicolons. Always use plain hyphen-minus (`-`); never use en dash or em dash. Keep every named function or method at most 100 physical lines; split it when it would exceed the limit.

**Trailing commas and separators**
On any multiline array literal, object literal, or call-site argument list, always include a trailing comma after the last item. On formal parameter lists, always include a trailing comma after the last parameter even on a single physical line (e.g. `function foo(bar: Baz,)`); omit only for empty parameter lists. Use commas to separate runtime lists (parameters, arguments, destructuring bindings, array/object elements). Use semicolons to separate members inside `interface` bodies and inline object types.

**Naming**
Use `camelCase` for variables, functions, and parameters. Use `UPPER_CASE` for hardcoded configuration literals (`const` bindings whose values are fixed literals: timeouts, limits, HTTP methods, status codes, path segments, feature flags) and for shared reusable Tailwind class name strings. Use `PascalCase` for classes, interfaces, and type aliases. Never prefix interfaces with `I`. Use `snake_case` bindings only when mirroring external schemas (REST/JSON payloads, database rows, vendor SDKs).

**Named constants - no magic numbers**
Never use a bare numeric literal directly in an expression. Always assign it to a named `UPPER_CASE` `const` first, then reference that binding in expressions. Exception: obvious pure-bookkeeping indices such as `index + 1`.

**Multiline formatting**
Always put one element per physical line in multiline array literals, object literals, and parameter lists. Always put one operand or sub-condition per physical line in every compound boolean predicate (`&&`, `||`) - applies to `if`, `while`, `do…while`, `for` conditions, ternary conditions, `const`/`let` boolean bindings, `return`, and `throw`. Always put one clause per physical line in `for` loop headers (initializer, condition, update). For non-empty function signatures, put each parameter on its own line, close with `): ReturnType {` on its own line. Always expand inline object type annotations (`{ … }`) across multiple lines with each `member: Type;` on its own line.

**Variables**
Never use `var`. Use `const` by default; use `let` only when a binding must be reassigned. Use `const` for destructuring patterns where no binding will be reassigned; use `let` when any binding from the pattern needs reassignment.

**Modules and imports**
Always use ES6 `import` / `export`. Never use CommonJS (`require`, `module.exports`, `exports`). Always use `import type { … }` for compile-time-only types. Always use `@`-prefixed path aliases for internal imports (e.g. `@types-internal/…`, `@config/…`, `@classNames/…`); never use relative ascending paths (`../`, `../../`). Register every alias in `compilerOptions.paths`. Do not manually reorder imports - run `npx eslint PATH_FILE --fix` (or `npx eslint --fix PATH1 PATH2` for multiple files). Prefer named exports; use `export default` only when a framework requires it (Next.js pages, layouts, route handlers). Handler and service modules: bottom `export { … }` block or inline exports at declaration - one style per file, no mixing. Framework entry files (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `route.ts`, etc.): inline `export const revalidate`, `export async function generateMetadata`, and `export default` are required. Never create convenience barrel `index.ts` re-export files unless a package boundary requires a stable public API surface.

**Post-edit validation**
After creating or modifying any `.ts` / `.tsx` file, agents must run `npx eslint --fix CHANGED_PATHS` on every changed path (or `npx eslint PATH_FILE --fix` per file), then `npx tsc --noEmit` on the project (not file paths - that ignores `tsconfig.json`). Use `-p path/to/tsconfig.json` when required. Fix all errors and re-run until both commands exit successfully. Never leave ESLint or TypeScript errors for the user.

**Types**
Declare all reusable `interface` and `type` alias shapes exclusively under `./src/types/**/*.ts`; import them via `@types-internal/…`. For React component types (`./src/types/components/**`), files are generalized per feature group - create one type file per feature group, not per component, using `PascalCase` directories and the `<FeatureGroup>-types.ts` naming pattern (e.g., `./src/types/components/UserProfile/UserProfile-types.ts`). Always write explicit `: string`, `: number`, or `: boolean` annotations on function parameters, locals, and `const`/`let` bindings - never rely on inference for primitives. Always annotate array bindings with an explicit element type (`T[]` or `Array<T>`). Prefer `interface` for reusable object shapes; use `type` when `interface` cannot express the shape (unions, discriminated unions, tuples, mapped types, conditional types). Never use `any`; use `unknown` for values not known statically, then narrow with type guards or schema validators immediately after ingestion. Always enable strict null checks and guard against `null` and `undefined` explicitly. Use string literal union types for bounded domains.

**Config files**
Declare all static behavioral configuration literals exclusively under `./src/config/**/*.ts`; import them via `@config/…`. For React component config (`./src/config/components/**`), files are generalized per feature group - create one config file per feature group, not per component, using `PascalCase` directories and the `<FeatureGroup>-config.ts` naming pattern (e.g., `./src/config/components/UserProfile/UserProfile-config.ts`). Never scatter `export const SOME_LITERAL = …` across services, handlers, or other files outside `./src/config/**`. Config is not content (rendered strings belong in `./src/content/**`); config is not state logic (reducers and state builders belong in `./src/states/**`); config is not presentation styling (Tailwind classes belong in `./src/styles/classNames/**`).

**User-facing content**
Declare all hardcoded static values that render as user-visible output - labels, headings, button text, descriptions, empty-state messages, nav text - exclusively under `./src/content/**/*.ts`; import them via `@content/…`. For React components, files are generalized per feature group - create one content file per feature group, not per component, using `PascalCase` directories and the `<FeatureGroup>-content.ts` naming pattern (e.g., `./src/content/Counter/Counter-content.ts`). Register `"@content/*": ["src/content/*"]` in `compilerOptions.paths`. Never inline these strings in source files or in config files.

**Pure state logic**
Declare all pure state transition functions - reducers, state factories, action builders, initial state builders, derived selectors, reset helpers - exclusively under `./src/states/**/*.ts`; import them via `@states/…`. For React components, files are generalized per feature group - create one state file per feature group, not per component, using `PascalCase` directories and the `<FeatureGroup>-state.ts` naming pattern (e.g., `./src/states/Counter/Counter-state.ts`). Register `"@states/*": ["src/states/*"]` in `compilerOptions.paths`. Never place JSX, React Hooks, or Context creation in state files.

**Module design**
Every `*.ts` / `*.tsx` module owns exactly one primary concern: fetch/transport, validate, transform/map, orchestrate, configure, define types, pure state transition logic, user-facing static content, UI view component, or reusable styling strings. Never mix concern types in one file. Name each file after its single concern. Never write HTTP I/O inside a validator or transformer. Never embed domain validation inside a fetch function. When the same logical step appears in two or more places, extract it once as a named function before writing it a second time - never paste duplicate blocks. For UI view components, each `.tsx` file declares and exports exactly one React component; a List component and its Item component are always split into separate files residing together in the same PascalCase feature directory (e.g., `ComponentNameList.tsx` and `ComponentName.tsx` under `./src/components/ComponentName/`). Reusable Tailwind class strings live exclusively under `./src/styles/classNames/**/*.ts`, are named in `PascalCase` matching the component or feature group with a `-classNames` suffix (e.g., `Button-classNames.ts`), and are imported via `@classNames/…`; single-use strings may remain inline in JSX.

**Conditionals**
Always use strict equality (`===` and `!==`). Never use loose equality (`==` or `!=`). In `switch` statements, always wrap each `case` body in `{ }` braces, always include a `default` branch that throws a named `Error`, and never fall through from one `case` to the next. Use a ternary only when both branches are single tokens, the result is assigned to a named binding, and the expression is not nested inside another ternary - never use a ternary as a statement. Use early-return guard clauses at the start of a function body to reject invalid inputs before business logic runs.

**Loops**
Always use `for` loops with an explicit initializer, condition, and update for counted iteration. `while` is permitted when loop control reads more clearly. Never use `for…of`, `for…in` as an array walker, or `.map` / `.forEach` / `.filter` for iteration over a collection.

**Functions**
Always write reusable behavior as named `function` or `async function` declarations at module scope. Never write a `function` or `async function` nested inside another function's body - move callees to module scope (declared below their callers) or import them. Callbacks passed to APIs (e.g. `Promise`, `addEventListener`, `setTimeout`) may be arrow or anonymous functions. Always structure every function body as three vertical bands: Data layer (destructure params, guards, input normalization) → Operation layer (business logic, branching, delegated calls) → Return. Always pass multiple related inputs as a single object annotated with an `interface` or `type` alias. Every `return` must pass a single identifier that already holds the outgoing value - never return a composite expression, a call, `await`, arithmetic, `&&`/`||` stacks, object/array literals, template literals, or ternaries directly. Every `throw` must throw only a `const`-bound `Error` identifier - never `throw new Error(…)` inline. Order functions top-to-bottom from higher abstraction to lower; define each file-local callee below its first caller.

**Asynchronous programming**
Always use `async` and `await`. Never use `.then()` or raw callback functions. Wrap potentially failing operations in `try…catch`; always assign `new Error(…)` to a `const` first, then `throw` that identifier. For parallel async operations, collect promises in an array using a `for` loop without `await`, then resolve them all at once with `await Promise.all(promises)`. For sequential async operations, use a `for` loop with `await` inside the body.

**Documentation**
Write JSDoc (`/** … */`) above every exported function and above any module-private function whose behavior, preconditions, side effects, or `throw` contract are non-obvious from the name and types alone. Use `@param`, `@returns`, and `@throws` tags; for object-parameter functions, document each field as `@param {Type} params.field`. Write inline comments (`//`) only to explain non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints the code cannot convey by itself. Never commit `/* */` block comments.
